WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.060 --> 00:00:05.360
This epic trench battle from "1917"

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:08.160
was shot to look like one long take.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:12.000
And it was a huge challenge
for both the cast and crew.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.030
It involved multiple cranes,

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:17.403
speeding trucks, and
perfectly timed handoffs.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:21.213
But this is just one
example of a one-take scene,

00:00:21.213 --> 00:00:22.121
or a "oner,"

00:00:22.121 --> 00:00:24.240
that pushed filmmakers to their limits

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:27.760
and required impressive
technical innovations.

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:30.230
Here's how eight movie
and TV scenes were shot

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:32.262
to look like one long take.

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:39.700
One-take scenes are naturally
suited for intense action,

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:43.280
as you can see in this fight
scene in "Atomic Blonde."

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:44.960
Despite shooting in a real building,

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:48.500
the crew still had to alter the
set to accommodate the oner.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:50.890
For example, they built this elevator,

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:53.370
which helped create a
natural stitch point,

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:55.430
a way to hide the moment the camera cuts

00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:58.130
and keep the continuous illusion going.

00:00:58.130 --> 00:01:00.100
They also used another stitching technique

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:02.750
called body crossing,
in which a cut is hidden

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:04.660
when a performer blocks the camera,

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:06.463
as seen here with this henchman.

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:11.980
Because the fight was shot
to look like one take,

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:14.700
it needed to be shot
in chronological order.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:16.940
So the effects team
had to constantly apply

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:19.750
new makeup to Charlize Theron
and the other characters

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:22.820
as the fighting continued
and injuries increased.

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:24.160
Charlize did her own stunts

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:25.680
for a large part of this sequence.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:27.430
However, for some of the riskier moves,

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:30.380
the crew subbed in her stunt
double, Monique Ganderton.

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:32.580
For example, the director used motion blur

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:34.540
when Charlize had to fall down the stairs

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:36.360
and slam into a wall.

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:38.330
That's actually Monique
tumbling down the stairs

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:40.880
before switching back to
Charlize hitting the wall.

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:48.540
Unlike "Atomic Blonde,"
Netflix's "Extraction"

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:50.710
hops from location to location,

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:52.583
as opposed to just one building.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:56.310
The entire sequence goes
on for about 12 minutes

00:01:56.310 --> 00:02:00.610
and consists of approximately
36 segments cobbled together.

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:03.530
The nonstop action required
director Sam Hargrave

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:05.600
to do some stunts of his own.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:07.640
For this car chase, he strapped himself

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:09.660
to the hood of another vehicle

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:11.630
and then had to quickly unstrap himself

00:02:11.630 --> 00:02:14.760
and place the camera inside the other car.

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:17.730
He then had to replicate
the same shot backwards

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:20.360
by leaning out the car once he was inside.

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:23.290
This helped create a
natural transition point.

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:25.960
If Sam's position was off even slightly,

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:29.030
they'd have to shoot it all over again.

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:30.930
Then for this shot,
where Chris Hemsworth's

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:32.888
stunt double tumbles off an awning,

00:02:32.888 --> 00:02:35.190
Sam roped himself into a pulley system

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:37.490
to get up close to the action.

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:40.170
The sequence also fools
the eye in many other ways.

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:42.580
For instance, when Ovi
runs through this doorway,

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:44.250
he is actually running into a room

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:46.500
in a different building 10 blocks away.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:48.155
The crew had to change locations

00:02:48.155 --> 00:02:49.655
to one where there was more space

00:02:49.655 --> 00:02:51.890
to pull off the upcoming action.

00:02:51.890 --> 00:02:54.180
And like with "Atomic
Blonde," they frequently

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:56.300
swapped out Chris with his stunt double,

00:02:56.300 --> 00:02:58.020
Bobby Holland Hanton.

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:00.400
The first shot you see
here is Chris' face.

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:03.220
They could then pan to his
double without anyone noticing

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:04.870
by shooting the back of his head.

00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:13.390
For the Day of the Dead
parade scene in "Spectre,"

00:03:13.390 --> 00:03:16.930
cinematographer Hoyte van
Hoytema wanted to start big

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:19.200
and then go small and intimate.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:21.730
That meant starting
wide with parade shots,

00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:23.590
then getting in closer to the marchers

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:26.450
before following Bond into a hotel room.

00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:29.110
To pull this off, Hoyte
used a technocrane.

00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:30.200
The crane started high

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:32.880
and then lowered down into
the crowd without cutting.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:35.140
A Steadicam operator then jumped off,

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:36.940
pushed farther into the crowd,

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:40.490
and followed actor Daniel
Craig up to the hotel room.

00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:42.940
While the street scenes
were shot in Mexico,

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:46.040
the hotel room was actually set in the UK.

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:49.770
The crowd scenes featured 1,500 extras.

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:53.300
According to ILM VFX
supervisor Mark Bakowski,

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:55.030
since they couldn't get a perfect shot

00:03:55.030 --> 00:03:58.230
where every extra looked the
right way at the right time,

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:01.863
the VFX team inserted digital
masks over their faces.

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:11.940
Sometimes oners are limited
by the space they're shot in,

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:15.200
which means the crew needs
to be a bit more creative.

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:18.210
In the season two premiere of
"The Marvelous Mrs. Maisel,"

00:04:18.210 --> 00:04:20.950
cinematographer M. David
Mullen had to lead the camera

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:22.890
through a fictional department store,

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:25.250
through a mail slot, and
then into the small room

00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:28.590
where Midge Maisel works
as a switchboard operator.

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:30.102
Unlike other continuous shots,

00:04:30.102 --> 00:04:33.230
there were no wide spaces for
the camera to pass through --

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:36.890
just five feet of space between
these two rows of actresses.

00:04:36.890 --> 00:04:38.970
For both cameraperson and performer,

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:41.070
it was about precise timing.

00:04:41.070 --> 00:04:44.050
The space was so tight that
the actresses had to lean back

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:46.000
so they wouldn't get hit by the camera.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:48.370
Then, when the camera went
through the center of the room,

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:50.210
actresses wheeled around in their chairs

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:51.760
so they wouldn't block the shot.

00:04:57.860 --> 00:05:00.290
For "Birdman," it wasn't just one scene,

00:05:00.290 --> 00:05:04.160
but the entire movie that needed
to look like one long take.

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:07.300
According to director
Alejandro González Iñárritu,

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:10.020
some of the takes were
up to 20 minutes long.

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:11.877
Because of the amount
of movement in the film,

00:05:11.877 --> 00:05:13.740
"Birdman" was shot using small

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:16.080
handheld cameras and Steadicams.

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:18.410
These allowed the crew to
more easily follow the cast

00:05:18.410 --> 00:05:21.390
as they walked through New
York's crowded streets.

00:05:21.390 --> 00:05:22.990
This also meant the crew couldn't use

00:05:22.990 --> 00:05:24.910
typical large light kits.

00:05:24.910 --> 00:05:27.970
So, according to cinematographer
Emmanuel Lubezki,

00:05:27.970 --> 00:05:29.730
they instead relied on natural

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:31.570
and practical lights in the scene,

00:05:31.570 --> 00:05:33.960
like these colorful light
bulbs above Michael Keaton

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:37.197
and even the lights
emitting from Times Square.

00:05:37.197 --> 00:05:39.630
"Birdman" wasn't just a
challenge for the crew;

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:42.030
it was also a huge endeavor for the cast.

00:05:42.030 --> 00:05:46.170
According to Emma Stone, if
she flubbed even a single word,

00:05:46.170 --> 00:05:48.473
she'd have to start
again from the very top.

00:05:54.070 --> 00:05:57.280
Like "Birdman," "1917" was
also filmed to look like

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:00.840
one single take over its
entire two-hour running time.

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:02.700
But it was even more complicated

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:05.380
due to its epic battle sequences.

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:06.810
Pulling this film off involved

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:09.360
the most careful planning imaginable.

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:11.730
Every set was precisely measured out

00:06:11.730 --> 00:06:13.030
and could be no longer than

00:06:13.030 --> 00:06:15.130
the length the camera could travel.

00:06:15.130 --> 00:06:17.040
Since the crew was constantly on foot,

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:19.850
they relied almost
entirely on natural light.

00:06:19.850 --> 00:06:21.210
Look at the scene where Schofield

00:06:21.210 --> 00:06:22.860
runs through a destroyed city.

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:25.110
It was lit up by flares in the sky.

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:26.720
Because the flares were the main light

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:28.980
for part of the scene,
the crew needed to time

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:32.030
exactly how long the flares
needed to be in the air.

00:06:32.030 --> 00:06:34.020
They figured this out in preproduction,

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:36.520
where they ran a light above
a mini model of the set

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:39.310
to see where exactly all
the shadows would fall.

00:06:39.310 --> 00:06:42.050
Of course, the film also
relied on stitch points,

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:43.810
like here, when Blake and Schofield

00:06:43.810 --> 00:06:45.410
walk through a black curtain.

00:06:45.410 --> 00:06:47.130
But most of its magic was accomplished

00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:49.810
using innovations in technology.

00:06:49.810 --> 00:06:52.030
For example, Schofield's journey downriver

00:06:52.030 --> 00:06:53.900
was captured by a drone.

00:06:53.900 --> 00:06:55.966
Meanwhile, lighter and smaller cameras

00:06:55.966 --> 00:06:57.658
allowed the crew to move through trenches

00:06:57.658 --> 00:07:00.210
and difficult terrain with ease.

00:07:00.210 --> 00:07:02.300
Then, to get across no man's land,

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:04.610
cinematographer Roger Deakins and the crew

00:07:04.610 --> 00:07:07.580
attached cameras to a
cable system above the set.

00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:10.010
This shot, where Schofield
runs through a destroyed city

00:07:10.010 --> 00:07:12.380
and jumps off a ledge and into a river,

00:07:12.380 --> 00:07:15.010
was too dangerous to do in real life.

00:07:15.010 --> 00:07:17.020
So the part of him
running was shot on set,

00:07:17.020 --> 00:07:19.540
while the jump was actually shot using CGI

00:07:19.540 --> 00:07:20.680
with a swapped background

00:07:20.680 --> 00:07:23.140
that allowed for a seamless transition.

00:07:23.140 --> 00:07:26.110
Shooting continuous takes
means it's hard to call "cut,"

00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:28.610
so it's not ideal for making mistakes.

00:07:28.610 --> 00:07:30.920
But this moment here,
where actor George MacKay

00:07:30.920 --> 00:07:33.228
runs alongside the trench and then trips,

00:07:33.228 --> 00:07:34.550
was not intentional.

00:07:34.550 --> 00:07:37.663
But in the end it felt natural,
so it stayed in the movie.

00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:44.497
The entire set for this episode of

00:07:44.497 --> 00:07:46.520
"The Haunting of Hill
House" had to be built

00:07:46.520 --> 00:07:49.517
specifically to accommodate a single shot.

00:07:49.517 --> 00:07:50.720
Take a look at this elevator.

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:52.778
It was designed to lower the cameraperson

00:07:52.778 --> 00:07:54.854
down to the next floor
and allow them to capture

00:07:54.854 --> 00:07:56.387
the shot of Olivia and Hugh

00:07:56.387 --> 00:07:59.070
running down the stairs without a break.

00:07:59.070 --> 00:08:01.040
Because the camera goes between Hill House

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:03.100
and the funeral home in the episode,

00:08:03.100 --> 00:08:05.290
the two stages were
actually built adjacent

00:08:05.290 --> 00:08:07.680
to each other and connected by a hallway.

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:10.020
This allowed the camera to
follow Hugh as he walked

00:08:10.020 --> 00:08:12.950
directly from the funeral
home into the house.

00:08:12.950 --> 00:08:15.120
Oners are frantic by nature,

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:18.110
and they involve a lot of
movement and setup changes.

00:08:18.110 --> 00:08:19.820
The "Hill House" set had places throughout

00:08:19.820 --> 00:08:21.610
for the crew and their equipment to hide

00:08:21.610 --> 00:08:24.210
whenever the camera
turned in their direction.

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:27.580
Like with "Birdman" and
"1917," "Hill House" also

00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:30.190
demonstrated the challenges
of lighting a oner.

00:08:30.190 --> 00:08:33.100
If an actor missed a cue,
they wouldn't be lit properly.

00:08:33.100 --> 00:08:34.890
So series creator Mike Flanagan

00:08:34.890 --> 00:08:37.360
came up with a unique solution.

00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:39.520
When he clapped these
two wood blocks together,

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:41.507
the cast member knew what to do.

00:08:41.507 --> 00:08:43.807
[blocks clapping]

00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:49.330
One-take scenes aren't just used to show

00:08:49.330 --> 00:08:51.180
movement from place to place.

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:52.690
They can also be really useful

00:08:52.690 --> 00:08:55.390
in showing the passage of time.

00:08:55.390 --> 00:08:57.920
This scene from the
Showtime series "Kidding"

00:08:57.920 --> 00:08:59.737
shows the character Shaina watching

00:08:59.737 --> 00:09:01.970
"Mr. Pickles' Puppet Time" on her TV

00:09:01.970 --> 00:09:05.240
as her life progresses
and improves around her.

00:09:05.240 --> 00:09:07.460
The shot captures five years of her life

00:09:07.460 --> 00:09:10.510
over the course of one
minute and 42 seconds.

00:09:10.510 --> 00:09:12.780
Because the camera had
to circle constantly

00:09:12.780 --> 00:09:14.440
around the room as it changed,

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:16.930
this endeavor was an absolute mad rush

00:09:16.930 --> 00:09:18.505
for the cast and crew.

00:09:19.670 --> 00:09:21.050
To show the passage of time,

00:09:21.050 --> 00:09:23.640
the camera operators
circled around the room.

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:25.140
Each area of the room would show

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:27.160
a new development in Shaina's life.

00:09:27.160 --> 00:09:29.650
So whenever the camera
wasn't focused on them,

00:09:29.650 --> 00:09:32.280
the 50 crew members had
to swap out furniture

00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:33.800
and bring in new props.

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:36.620
Then this wall slid over into the kitchen.

00:09:36.620 --> 00:09:38.850
While that was happening,
actress Riki Lindhome

00:09:38.850 --> 00:09:41.350
had to go through
multiple costume changes,

00:09:41.350 --> 00:09:44.750
some of which involved
using a body double.

00:09:44.750 --> 00:09:47.250
What are some of your
favorite one-take scenes?

00:09:47.250 --> 00:09:49.583
Let us know in the comments.

